---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base title="Performance - FastQC">
  <p class="label">Benchmarks</p>
  <h1>Performance</h1>
  <p style="color: var(--ink-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem">
    Single-threaded, one file (7.3 GB Illumina WES). Pipelines typically parallelise across files
    via an external scheduler (Nextflow, Snakemake), so single-file throughput is the relevant metric.
  </p>

  <div class="grid-2" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
    <div>
      <h3 class="bench-title">Wall clock time</h3>
      <div class="bench-chart">
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label">Java</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-java" style="width: 100%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">9m 46s</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label">Rust</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-rust" style="width: 33%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">3m 13s</span>
          <span class="bench-speedup">3.0x faster</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="bench-title">Peak memory (RSS)</h3>
      <div class="bench-chart">
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label">Java</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-java" style="width: 100%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">444 MB</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label">Rust</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-rust" style="width: 6.3%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">28 MB</span>
          <span class="bench-speedup">16x less</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
    <a href={`${base}about/performance/report/`} class="btn btn-primary">
      Full benchmark report
    </a>
  </div>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem">
    <h2>Where the speed comes from</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem">
      Three specific optimisations, each identified through CPU profiling:
    </p>
    <div class="grid-3">
      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 1rem; margin-top: 0">SIMD adapter search</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--ink-muted)">
          The <code>memchr</code> crate uses ARM NEON / SSE to compare 16 bytes per cycle.
          Adapter search drops from 35% to 6% of runtime.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 1rem; margin-top: 0">Lookup-table base counting</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--ink-muted)">
          A 256-byte compile-time table replaces unpredictable branch chains,
          halving time spent in per-base modules.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 style="font-size: 1rem; margin-top: 0">Zero per-read allocation</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--ink-muted)">
          In-place uppercase conversion, zero-copy tile parsing, byte-slice tracking.
          No garbage collection overhead.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem">
    <h2>Multi-threading</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem">
      When a file gets two or more threads, it runs through a pipeline: a reader thread parses
      records and hands batches to analysis workers, each of which owns a subset of the QC
      modules. Every module still sees every read in order on one thread, so output is the same
      at any thread count. Without <code>-t</code>, FastQC-Rust uses the available CPUs, up to 6.
    </p>
    <div style="margin-bottom: 1rem; max-width: 36rem">
      <h3 class="bench-title">Wall clock time by <code>-t</code>, one 7.9 GB WES file (M1 Pro)</h3>
      <div class="bench-chart">
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-wide">-t 1</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-rust" style="width: 100%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">137.6 s</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-wide">-t 2</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-rust" style="width: 87%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">119.7 s</span>
          <span class="bench-speedup">1.15x</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-wide">-t 3</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-rust" style="width: 63%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">87.1 s</span>
          <span class="bench-speedup">1.58x</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-wide">-t 4</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-rust" style="width: 51%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">69.7 s</span>
          <span class="bench-speedup">1.98x</span>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-row">
          <span class="bench-label bench-label-wide">-t 6</span>
          <div class="bench-bar bench-bar-rust" style="width: 42%"></div>
          <span class="bench-time">58.3 s</span>
          <span class="bench-speedup">2.36x</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem">
      A single file stops getting faster at around <code>-t 6</code>. The heaviest module,
      Adapter Content, ends up alone on a worker and sets the pace, so more workers don't help.
      Past that point, extra cores are better spent on more files at once.
    </p>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
      For <code>.fastq.gz</code> input, decompression runs on its own thread via
      <a href="https://crates.io/crates/rapidgzip-core">rapidgzip-core</a> with a
      <a href="https://crates.io/crates/zlib-rs">zlib-rs</a> inflate, so it overlaps with the
      analysis. One decoder keeps up with the full pipeline; rapidgzip can
      decode a file in parallel chunks (<code>--decompress-threads</code>), but on typical data
      that adds CPU and memory without making the run faster.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 3rem">
    <h2>Test dataset</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
      119 GB across 11 files: Illumina WES, Element AVITI, ONT PromethION long-read, and BAM.
      Apple Silicon (aarch64-apple-darwin).
      The <a href={`${base}about/performance/report/`}>full report</a> has per-file results,
      memory profiles, a CPU breakdown, and analysis of whether these optimisations could be back-ported to Java.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
.bench-title {
  font-family: var(--font-body);
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  color: var(--ink-muted);
  margin-top: 0;
  margin-bottom: 0.625rem;
}
.bench-row {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.75rem;
  margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
}
.bench-label {
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  width: 2.5rem;
  flex-shrink: 0;
  color: var(--ink-muted);
}
.bench-label-wide {
  width: 2.75rem;
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
}
.bench-bar {
  height: 1.5rem;
  border-radius: 3px;
  min-width: 4px;
}
.bench-bar-java {
  background: var(--color-java);
}
.bench-bar-rust {
  background: var(--brand-maroon);
}
.bench-time {
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  color: var(--ink-muted);
  white-space: nowrap;
  flex-shrink: 0;
}
.bench-speedup {
  font-family: var(--font-heading);
  font-size: 0.875rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  color: var(--brand-maroon);
  white-space: nowrap;
  flex-shrink: 0;
}
</style>
